# Repository summary

## Purpose

`humblebundle-ebook-downloader` is a Node.js command-line program for downloading ebook files from purchases in a user's Humble Bundle account. It can select one bundle interactively or process all matching bundles, filter purchases by title, choose a file format, verify MD5 checksums, organize files into bundle directories, and generate simple HTML indexes.

The package is version `0.0.1`. The most recent repository commit is from July 18, 2017.

## Layout

- `index.js` contains the complete application: command-line parsing, authentication headers, API requests, filtering, prompts, downloading, checksums, caching, and HTML generation.
- `package.json` defines the package metadata, dependencies, and `npm start` command.
- `README.md` contains the original installation and usage documentation.
- `.gitignore` excludes local generated content.

There is no application source directory, automated test suite, lock file, lint configuration, or continuous-integration configuration. `npm test` is only a placeholder that exits with an error.

## Original data flow

The downloader accepts the value of Humble Bundle's legacy `_simpleauth_sess` cookie through `--auth_token`. It then performs two types of authenticated request:

1. `GET https://www.humblebundle.com/api/v1/user/order?ajax=true` returns the user's order keys.
2. `GET https://www.humblebundle.com/api/v1/order/{gamekey}?ajax=true` returns the details and downloadable files for each order.

The resulting array is cached as `/tmp/humblecache.json`. `--read_cache` bypasses the network and reads this file.

For each selected order, the program expects this legacy structure:

```text
order
  product
    human_name
  subproducts[]
    human_name
    downloads[]
      machine_name
      download_struct[]
        name
        md5
        url.web
```

The download implementation itself is independent of the order-list endpoint. If an adapter supplies objects in this structure, most of `fetch_books()` can continue to work.

## Command-line behavior

Important options include:

- `--auth_token`: legacy `_simpleauth_sess` cookie value.
- `--download_folder`: destination directory; defaults to `download`.
- `--format`: requested format; defaults to `EPUB`.
- `--title_matches`: case-insensitive purchase-title filter.
- `--download_limit`: concurrent download count; defaults to 5.
- `--read_cache`: use `/tmp/humblecache.json` rather than querying Humble Bundle.
- `--checksum`: verify downloaded files using the provided MD5 value.
- `--bundle`: create a directory for each bundle.
- `--all`: process every matching bundle without prompting.
- `--html`: generate a basic HTML index.
- `--disable`: refresh or organize existing files without downloading missing files.

## Related local material

The sibling `../dl` repository contains `hb.orders.ts`, a newer Deno-based order collector that used the same retired `/api/v1` endpoints.

The sibling `../hb-dl.orders` directory contains historical API snapshots. The October 22, 2026 snapshot has:

- 2,056 order records.
- 1,292 orders containing downloads.
- Approximately 69 MB of legacy JSON.

The November 7, 2026 output is zero bytes, consistent with the endpoint removal. The October snapshot is a useful fixture for regression testing a compatibility adapter.

## Maintenance concerns

- Both order-discovery endpoints were removed in November 2026.
- The legacy cookie no longer authenticates the redesigned account application by itself.
- Dependencies and Node.js idioms date from approximately 2015–2017.
- Download requests do not explicitly validate HTTP status codes or redirects before treating a stream as a file.
- The checksum reader uses the deprecated `Buffer` constructor and only MD5.
- Error handling can report completion even after individual failures.
- The fixed `/tmp/humblecache.json` path is not portable to standard Windows environments.
- Bundle downloads launched by `--all` are not globally constrained by one concurrency queue.
- HTML output is generated with string concatenation and has no dedicated escaping layer beyond selective tag stripping.

## Recommended modernization boundary

Keep acquisition separate from downloading:

```text
Humble authentication and library collector
                    |
                    v
          normalized legacy orders
                    |
                    v
        existing filtering/downloader logic
```

The first migration should replace only order acquisition and normalization. Broader dependency and download-engine modernization can then be performed independently and tested against the saved October 2026 order snapshot.
